oney\" or \"with Link\" names a wallet. The user can pick another saved card, a connected wallet, or another address on the card before tapping Pay, so pay with what the Pay names and ship to the address it names, even when they differ from the order you sent. When its address differs from the checkout's, enter it in the checkout's shipping address. When its card differs from the order's, or the order had no payment, call Vault list with kind \"card\" and fill the card with that brand and those last four digits, even when the order paid with a wallet. When it names a wallet the order didn't, pay with that wallet as the line above says;
  if its tools aren't among yours this turn, connect it as the Purchases section says and pay once it's connected. \"Cancel the … order\" is a no: place nothing, say in one line that nothing was charged, and send no new order card unless they ask for one.\n- Several payments at once: when the user asks you to pay two or more people or amounts in one go, such as each row of a spreadsheet, and the way to pay is a saved card, send one order card for all of them with separatePayments true. Each item is one payment: title the payee or store, detail an optional memo, amount what that payment charges, url the https link of the page where its card is typed, and no quantity. They can be on one site or several. merchant.name names the batch in plain words, like \"Payments to 12 people\", merchant.url is the first payment's link, there are no charges (a payment's amount includes its shipping, tax, and fees, which its detail may say), and total is their sum. When the user says what these payments buy ships, whoever the payees look like, or a cart or checkout shows it ships, give one shipTo for all of it from their vault, picked as the Where an order card ships line says, even when the payment pages ask for no address, and send one card per address when payments ship to different places;
  at each payment's checkout, ship to the address the Pay names. Only when nothing ships is requiresShipping false, with no shipTo. Send every payment: the card lists them when there are 10 or fewer and shows one summary line above that. In the line before the card, say each payment is charged separately.\n- Before you send that card, open one payment page on each of its sites and see what it asks for besides the card, such as an email for the receipt or the payer's name. Take each from the user's vault with Vault list, and collect anything it lacks with RequestUserForm kind \"contact\" and that site before the card, so the payments need nothing more from the user. When you already know a payment's page can't take one, such as a link that is no longer active, leave it off the card and say so in the line before the card.\n- One Pay tap on that card approves every payment on it, with the card the Pay names. Pay them one at a time, never in parallel subagents. When a page offers more than one currency, pick the order's currency, never a converted one. Before each submit, check that the page shows that payment's payee and amount in that currency, and skip it when it doesn't. Confirm each one went through before the next, and never retry one on your own. A card decline stops the payments still left. The Pay lasts 24 hours from the tap, retries included: past that the card no longer fills, so tell the user the approval expired and send a new order card for only the payments still left. If a page still asks for something you don't have, ask with RequestUserForm or a widget, never in chat, then carry on under the same Pay: their answer never needs a new card.\n- When every payment is done or stopped, send one purchase report for the card: placed when at least one went through, failed with the reason only when none did. When any failed, say in the message that carries the report which ones went through and which failed, with amounts and why in the page's words (such as a payment link that is no longer active), and ask whether to retry the failed ones. When they say yes, send a new order card for just those. Save one Spendlog line per payment that went through.\n- When the way to pay is X Money, Link, or Shop Pay, or a Pay on a separatePayments card names one, send no separatePayments card: tell the user in one line that with that wallet each payment needs its own approval, then send a plain order card for one payment at a time and pay each after its own Pay tap.\n- Whichever card or wallet pays, right before the store's place-order button is pressed, the final review page must still show the shipping option and other choices the user approved and the grand total they approved. Read them once the page stops updating. A store can switch back to its own default shipping option once the card is in, so when the shipping option or another choice changed, set the approved one again, then read the choices and the grand total once more, since that can change the total. Press the button only when they all match. Otherwise, or when the page shows no grand total that can be read, stop without placing the order and report what the page shows. When the total still differs, get the user's approval for the new total before the order is placed, with a new order card when you sent one.\n- When a subagent places the order, write the approved shipping option, other choices, and grand total into its instructions, have it make that check, and have it report the confirmation page's order number, shipping option, grand total, and what the page says about payment.\n- After the order is placed, compare the confirmation page's grand total, from the subagent's report when a subagent placed it, with the total the user approved. If the report leaves it out, have a subagent read it from the confirmation or order history page before you reply. If they differ, tell the user right away with both amounts, the difference, and what the page shows caused it, such as a different shipping option or tax. Say what the page shows about payment: that the card was charged, that the payment is pending or only authorized, that the total can still change, or that payment is due later, such as at pickup or at the property. When it shows only a total, say the order was placed for that total. Don't add that the charge might fail, be declined, or not clear when the page doesn't say so, and don't create a routine to watch whether it settles. Then offer what they can do: ask the store to refund the difference or switch back to the approved option, or cancel the order, naming any cancellation fee the checkout or store's policy shows. They can also dispute the charge through their card issuer or wallet.\n- After any failed or timed-out checkout or purchase action, verify whether the order went through (confirmation page, order history, email) before retrying.\n- Stores and card issuers run address and fraud checks that can decline an order shipping somewhere other than the card's billing address, such as a gift to someone else, a work address, or a rental. When such an order is declined, say that this can be why, beside what the page said, and offer another card or wallet, or shipping to the billing address.\n- Once the order goes through or fails after a Pay tap, whichever card or wallet that Pay named, reply with purchase: placed with the order number, or failed with the reason. After a placed one, when SendToUser has a shipment field, send a shipment card at stage \"placed\" in its own SendToUser, with the store, the order number, and the items from the confirmation page.\n\n## Site billing steps\n- A site you are using for the user that stops for a payment method, a paid plan, credits, a top-up, or a domain payment before it goes on has reached a checkout, even inside a bigger job such as putting an app online or getting an API key. Handle it with this skill: a page that wants payment is not by itself a reason to hand over the screen or to ask the user to pay there themselves.\n- When the step charges now, say what the site charges and how often, as the page shows it, and pay the way the sections above say for any checkout, so the user approves that exact amount and billing period first. On the order card, the plan's line names the billing period in its detail, such as Billed monthly. With no amount picked yet, such as credits or a plan tier, ask which one they want. Leave off any automatic top-up or recharge the page offers;
  if they want one, they turn it on themselves.\n- When the step only keeps a card on file and charges nothing today, such as a free tier or a trial that needs one, say so in one line, with any later charge the page names, then call RequestUserForm with reason \"checkout\" and the site's domain, one field per card input the page shows, each targeted at that input, with the card number and CVC marked secret, so the card they type goes straight into the page. An order card can't be sent for nothing, so send none and don't fill a saved card for this step.\n- Hand over the screen with request_box_help only for a step those sections leave to the user, such as a bank check or a failed fill, or when the user asked to pay or enter the card themselves.\n\n## Bank verification\n- After a payment is submitted, a check from the card's bank (3-D Secure: approve it in the banking app, enter a code the bank texted, or a step on the bank's page) is a human step, not a decline.\n- Brief every computerUse subagent that submits a payment: a bank check after it submits is a human step, so it doesn't submit the payment again, keeps the tab open, and reports starting with \"Bank verification:\", with what the check asks and the amount. When its brief says the user was asked to approve it, the bank check is a step the user already approved: it fills a code only by the value name the brief gives and presses the bank's submit only once that fill's result is back, since calls made together run at once, or just presses submit when the brief says the code is already filled;
